{
  "id": "collinearity-vif",
  "level": 3,
  "order": 5,
  "title": "Collinearity and the VIF",
  "summary": "When predictors overlap strongly, their separate effects are hard to estimate; the variance inflation factor measures that overlap.",
  "minutes": 7,
  "prerequisites": [
    "discriminant-validity"
  ],
  "body": [
    {
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Each endogenous construct is regressed on its predictors. If predictors correlate strongly, the data contain little information about their separate effects: standard errors grow and coefficients can even change sign. This is [[collinearity]]."
    },
    {
      "kind": "formula",
      "display": "VIFⱼ = 1 / (1 − R²ⱼ)",
      "explanation": "R²ⱼ is the R² from regressing predictor j on the other predictors of the same outcome. VIF = 1 means no overlap; VIF = 5 means 80% of predictor j is explained by the others."
    },
    {
      "kind": "demo",
      "demo": "vif",
      "initial": {
        "r2": 0.716
      },
      "caption": "Move R²ⱼ and watch the VIF explode as it approaches 1."
    },
    {
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Hair et al. (2022) treat VIF values of 5 or more as critical, 3–5 as possibly problematic and below 3 as ideal; these are heuristics. The [[vif]] is checked for predictor constructs in the structural model and for indicators of Mode B blocks. It is not a criterion for reflective indicators, which should correlate. In the poor-discriminant model, A and B both predict C with VIF = 3.52 and both get small f² values (.02 and .03)."
    }
  ],
  "mistake": {
    "title": "Computing VIF for reflective indicators",
    "text": "High correlations among reflective indicators are what you want. Deleting reflective items because of their VIF lowers reliability for no reason."
  },
  "tryIt": {
    "task": "Run PLS-SEM on the poor-discriminant data and find the structural VIFs of A and B as predictors of C. Then read their paths and f² values.",
